---

<Base title="5. Nullability & optionality — C# vs TypeScript" description="5. Nullability & optionality: side-by-side C# and TypeScript examples." slug="nullability">
  <div slot="header" id="top">
    <p><small><a href="/">C# vs TS</a> / 5. Nullability & optionality</small></p>
    <h1>5. Nullability &amp; optionality</h1>
    <p><strong>How to read:</strong> left is C#, middle is TypeScript, right is blank Mystery (yours). Narrow screens stack.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="nullability">
  
  <h2>5. Nullability &amp; optionality</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>#nullable enable

string definitely = "hi";
// definitely = null; // warning

string? maybe = null;
int? count = null; // Nullable&lt;int&gt;

string result = maybe ?? "fallback";
string upper = maybe?.ToUpper() ?? "N/A";
ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(maybe);

int len = maybe?.Length ?? 0;
int v = count.GetValueOrDefault();
bool has = count.HasValue;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>let definitely: string = "hi";
// definitely = null; // error under strict

let maybe: string | null = null;
let missing: string | undefined; // unassigned
let count: number | null = null;

let result = maybe ?? "fallback";
let upper = maybe?.toUpperCase() ?? "N/A";
if (maybe == null) throw new Error("required");

let len = maybe?.length ?? 0;
let v = count ?? 0;
let has = count != null;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <h3>5a. Optional members + null-forgiving</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>public class User &#123; public string? Nick &#123; get; set; &#125; &#125;
User u = new();
string label = u.Nick ?? "anon";

string? input = GetInput();
int l1 = input!.Length; // "trust me"
if (input is not null) &#123; int l2 = input.Length; &#125;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>type User = &#123; nick?: string &#125;; // string | undefined
const u: User = &#123;&#125;;
const label = u.nick ?? "anon";

let input: string | null = getInput();
let l1 = input!.length; // "trust me" — same risk
if (input !== null) &#123; let l2 = input.length; &#125;

// optional chaining + call + index:
let f = input?.length;
let r = obj?.method?.();
let el = arr?.[0];</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li><code>string?</code> (C#) ≈ <code>string | null</code> (TS). TS object properties/params usually need <code>| undefined</code> too (<code>nick?: string</code>).</li>
    <li><code>?. ?? ??= !</code> exist in both. TS <code>== null</code> checks both null and undefined — the idiomatic “missing” check.</li>
    <li>Prefer <code>??</code> over <code>||</code> in TS: <code>||</code> also falls through on <code>0/""</code>/<code>false</code>, <code>??</code> only on null/undefined.</li>
  </ul>

  </section>

